You are given an array containing n integers. Your task is to determine the longest increasing subsequence in the array, i.e., the longest subsequence where every element is larger than the previous one.
A subsequence is a sequence that can be derived from the array by deleting some elements without changing the order of the remaining elements.

Input:
The first line contains an integer n: the size of the array.
After this there are n integers x_1,x_2,...,x_n: the contents of the array.

Output:
Print the length of the longest increasing subsequence.

Constraints:
1<=n<=2*10^5
1<=x_i<=10^9

Strategy:
Here a segment tree can be used since we can just rank the numbers and the add them into the segment tree from the smallest to the biggest. The segment tree will naturally keep the order since we will index them by rank and therefore it will by building the subsequence being able to find where to put a value in log N and update the total count of elements in the corresponding subsequence. This is done because we are adding bigger and bigger numbers into the segment tree. They are non decreasing and therefore it is guaranteed to contain only <= numbers. When we get to a new number we ask whats the biggest value (length of IS) in the range (0, rank[new]) then we add 1 to that and update the segment tree. The only thing we need to handle here are duplicate values. Since a duplicate value after the same value has already been added to the segment tree would just append itself to the subsequence which ends with the same value we need to make sure this doesn't happen. We can do this by reversing the order in which we process numbers that are the same. This is actually the same logic used in DP to get single uses out of elements (in 0/1 knapsack for example). This works because when we go through the number we will give the ranges to the segment tree in the opposite way which means it will not have yet put in the duplicate numbers inside that range, for the next duplicate the range will get smaller and therefore will not see the outer duplicate. We can achieve this just by making all the indices negative in the pairs, since the index is used as a tiebreaker if we reverse them (by making them negative), the order of the duplicates will get reversed. Since we go from biggest to smallest number and the biggest number is not necessarily contained in the LIS we need to keep track of a global maximum of all values saved to the segment tree and we output that at the end.
The second approach is using an active array. Here for each index we will save the smallest value that is at the end of a sequence for the IS of length i in active[i]. Then we can use binary search for the DP transition. We iterate through the array by size from 0 to n-1 (n-1 --> length n) and for each length we just try to append the new number somewhere. There are only 2 options we care about, either the number will be all the way at the end (meaning we can append it to any sequence and therefore the maximum length will increase) or it will be somewhere else. If it is at the end we just use active[i]=active[i-1] but if it is somewhere else we need to find the best sequence to append it to (maximizing length and do it) this we do by finding the sequence which ends in a number smaller then our current and then using that count+1 for active[i]. This is the definition of a lower bound. We use lower_bound(arr.begin(), arr.end(), current) and whatever is the output is the subsequence that is closest to our value but >= which means we will replace the smallest value which was >= therefore minimizing the gap between numbers and allowing the next one to be easier to append. This is the best strategy since if the number cannot be appended to the end meaning it cannot extend all the subsequences we append it to the one for which the gain is the biggest and since the best subsequence will be the one with the smallest gaps we choose that one. Since the active array keeps track of the last number of the subsequence of length i for the final answer we just output active.size().